/*!
    \page qtbridges-index.html
    \title Qt Bridges Documentation
    \brief Build Qt Quick applications with a backend written in your own language.

    \note
        Qt Bridges (C# and Rust) is currently under Technology Preview, and
        under development, which means that APIs, supported platforms,
        and requirements might change in future releases.
        The rest of the bridges (Java/Kotlin, Swift, and Python) are currently on
        alpha state.

    \section1 What is Qt Bridges?

    \quotation
        \e {What if you could write Qt Quick UI backends in your favorite
        programming language without knowing much of Qt API?}
    \endquotation

    That question shaped the goal of Qt Bridges: to bring Qt Quick to many more
    languages, in a way that feels natural in each of them. Complete
    \l{https://wiki.qt.io/Language_Bindings}{Qt language bindings} are valuable,
    but exposing every \l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtmodules.html}{Qt module}
    takes a long time, and it asks you to embrace Qt and C++ idioms. Qt Bridges
    takes a narrower path. It exposes the data and logic of your backend to a
    Qt Quick user interface, and leaves the rest of Qt out.

    In practice, you annotate classes that hold your data and functionality, and
    Qt Bridges makes them available to QML. This includes feeding
    \l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qabstractitemmodel.html}{QAbstractItemModel}-based
    views without writing the model code yourself.

    \section1 How it works

    A Qt Bridges application has two parts. The \b{user interface} is written
    in QML and is the same whichever language you use. The \b{backend} is
    written in your language and holds the data and logic. The bridge connects
    them: you mark the types that QML should see, using the mechanism of your
    language (attributes, annotations, or decorators), and the UI can then read
    their properties, call their methods, react to their signals, and show their
    data in lists and tables.

    \image qtbridges-architecture.svg {Qt Bridges architecture: a QML user interface, the Qt Bridges layer, and a backend written in your language}

    Because the UI depends only on what the backend exposes, you can change one
    side without touching the other, or reuse the same QML with another
    language.

    \section2 Key concepts

    \table
    \header
        \li Term
        \li Meaning
    \row
        \li Property
        \li A value of a backend object that QML can read and, when it is
            writable, change. QML updates automatically when it changes.
    \row
        \li Method
        \li A function of a backend object that QML can call, for example when
            a button is clicked.
    \row
        \li Signal
        \li A notification emitted by the backend to tell QML that something happened.
    \row
        \li Model
        \li A backend type that provides a collection of items to views such as
            \c ListView and \c TableView.
    \row
        \li Binding
        \li A QML expression that keeps a UI value in sync with other values,
            such as a backend property.
    \endtable

    \section1 Supported languages

    Each language has its own bridge, with its own requirements and
    documentation. C#/.NET and Rust are in Technology Preview and are under
    active development. Java/Kotlin, Python, and Swift are in an alpha state:
    they work as a preview of the approach, and are not under active
    development at the moment.

    \table
    \header
        \li Language
        \li Status
        \li Platforms
        \li Requirements
    \row
        \li \l{Qt Bridges - C#}{C#/.NET}
        \li \b{Technology Preview}
        \li Windows (x64)
        \li .NET 8 or later
    \row
        \li \l{Qt Bridges - Rust}{Rust}
        \li \b{Technology Preview}
        \li Linux (x86_64), Windows (x64), macOS (arm64, experimental)
        \li Rust 1.87 or later
    \row
        \li \l{Qt Bridges - Java/Kotlin}{Java/Kotlin}
        \li Alpha
        \li See the language documentation
        \li OpenJDK 21 or later, Gradle 8.14.2 or later
    \row
        \li \l{Qt Bridges - Python}{Python}
        \li Alpha
        \li See the language documentation
        \li Python 3.9 or later
    \row
        \li \l{Qt Bridges - Swift}{Swift}
        \li Alpha
        \li macOS 14 or later (Apple Silicon)
        \li Swift 6.0 or later
    \endtable

    \section1 Getting started

    Pick your language to see how to install Qt Bridges and create your first
    application. For now, you need a Qt 6.10 or later installation on your
    system. The 1.0 release will include its dependencies in each package.

    \b{In Technology Preview:}

    \grid
        \griditem {qtbridges-csharp-index.html} {
            Get started with Qt Bridges for C#/.NET
        }
        \griditem {qtbridges-rust-index.html} {
            Get started with Qt Bridges for Rust
        }
    \endgrid

    \b{Alpha state:}

    \grid
        \griditem {qtbridges-java-index.html}{Java/Kotlin} {
            Get started with Qt Bridges for Java and Kotlin
        }
        \griditem {qtbridges-python-index.html}{Python} {
            Get started with Qt Bridges for Python
        }
        \griditem {qtbridges-swift-index.html}{Swift} {
            Get started with Qt Bridges for Swift
        }
    \endgrid

    \section2 New to Qt Quick?

    If you have not written any QML before, the
    \l{Starter layouts for Qt Quick desktop applications}{starter layouts} give
    you ready-to-run window layouts and a comparison of Qt Quick Controls styles
    to build on.

    \section1 Designing your application

    These guidelines apply to any language:

    \list
        \li Keep the UI declarative. Describe what the user sees in QML, and
            keep application logic in the backend.
        \li Expose only what the UI needs. Everything else stays private to
            the backend.
        \li Expose lists and tables as models, instead of many separate
            properties.
        \li Put each page or reusable part of the UI in its own \c{.qml} file.
    \endlist

    The language-specific pages show the project structure and the templates
    available to create it.

    \section1 Limitations

    Qt Bridges is not a complete binding of the Qt API:

    \list
        \li Many Qt Quick modules contain C++ APIs that are not available.
        \li Qt classes are not exposed to your language, so you cannot create or
            subclass classes such as \c QWidget from the backend.
        \li Other Qt modules, such as networking or multimedia, are not
            wrapped. Use the libraries of your language instead.
    \endlist

    If you need the complete Qt API, consider the
    \l{https://wiki.qt.io/Language_Bindings}{Qt language bindings} or Qt for C++.

    \section1 Licensing and contributing

    The C#/.NET and Rust bridges are released under Qt Commercial and
    \l{https://www.gnu.org/licenses/lgpl-3.0.html}{LGPLv3} licenses. For
    commercial licensees, Qt Bridges is Pre-Release Code under the Qt License
    Agreement. The \c README of each repository has the exact terms, including
    third-party software.

    Report issues in the
    \l{https://qt-project.atlassian.net/browse/QTBRIDGES}{Qt Bug Tracker},
    and discuss your questions or suggestion in the
    \l{https://forum.qt.io/category/78/qt-bridges}{Qt Forum} or in our
    \l{https://discord.gg/WNdGHnHagP}{Discord community}.
*/
